# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; never commit real secrets.
#
#   cp .env.example .env

########################################################################
# Required
########################################################################

# PostgreSQL password for the engine's own database user.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me

# Admin password for the engine's REST API and Administrator UI.
#
# OIE 4.6.0 seeds admin/admin and cannot take a chosen initial password from
# config, so the `bootstrap` compose service rotates the account to this value
# on first boot. Set it before the first `compose up`.
OIE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-too

########################################################################
# Optional -- shown with their defaults
########################################################################

# Engine version. Bump both together: the checksum comes from the `sha256sums`
# asset attached to the GitHub release.
#OIE_VERSION=4.6.0
#OIE_SHA256=7c82e79027e671277e1d78d0f7bbb1c53ddf1be476f1e80c2ddbfcf7855900ea
#OIE_IMAGE=oie/engine:4.6.0

# Trim the image: the Administrator client jars are ~82MB and only needed if
# operators launch the Administrator GUI from this server; the mccommand CLI is
# ~32MB and superseded by the REST API here.
#INCLUDE_ADMIN_CLIENT=true
#INCLUDE_CLI=false

# Where the API is published. Loopback by default -- put the proxy overlay or
# your own ingress in front before exposing it off-box.
#OIE_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
#OIE_HTTPS_PORT=8443

# Guards appdata/keystore.jks, which holds the engine's self-signed TLS cert and
# its data-encryption secret key. The value shipped in mirth.properties is
# identical in every 4.6.0 install, so override it -- but note that changing it
# after first boot makes the existing keystore unreadable.
#KEYSTORE_PASSWORD=changeme

# JVM heap. The engine's own default is a 256m ceiling, which is too small for
# anything real.
#OIE_HEAP_MAX=1g
#OIE_HEAP_MIN=

# Database pool sizes. The engine opens two pools of this size (read/write and
# read-only), so keep POSTGRES_MAX_CONNECTIONS above 2x this x replicas.
#DATABASE_MAX_CONNECTIONS=20
#DATABASE_READONLY_MAX_CONNECTIONS=20
#POSTGRES_MAX_CONNECTIONS=200

#POSTGRES_DB=mirthdb
#POSTGRES_USER=mirthdb
#POSTGRES_IMAGE_TAG=18.3-alpine

# Set to a port number to re-enable the plaintext admin listener. Off by
# default; the REST API is HTTPS-only either way.
#HTTP_PORT=0

#TZ=UTC

########################################################################
# Optional -- licensed / third-party extensions
########################################################################

# Extension zips are picked up two ways:
#   1. drop them in ./extensions (mounted read-only, see extensions/README.md)
#   2. fetch them at boot, optionally with an enforced checksum:
#        sha256:<hex>@<url>
#
# Pin the checksum for anything from outside your own infrastructure: these
# archives hold jars and WARs the engine loads into its own JVM. Startup aborts
# on a mismatch and warns when a URL has no checksum.
#
# Enables the browser-based web administrator at /oie-webadmin/ (docs/web-ui.md):
#OIE_EXTENSION_URLS=sha256:11014e5fc2a2b9ca5ef5c6750c42fc65ce832b3f7e918e076f5e1f9fa71fbf9a@https://github.com/gibson9583/oie-web-support-plugin/releases/download/v1.0.3/websupport-1.0.3.zip
#
# A private registry needs an auth header; comma-separate multiple URLs:
#OIE_DOWNLOAD_HEADER=PRIVATE-TOKEN: your-token

# Extension compatibility is an EXACT version match, so an extension built for
# Mirth Connect 4.5.x does not load on OIE 4.6.0 -- it is refused outright and
# every channel using its connectors becomes an invalid channel. This rewrites
# the <mirthVersion> of extensions you installed to the running version.
# Ask the vendor for a 4.6.0-tagged build first; read extensions/README.md
# before turning this on.
#OIE_EXTENSION_RETAG_VERSION=false

########################################################################
# Optional -- single sign-on (OpenID Connect)
########################################################################

# SSO is configured in the console, not here: Settings > OIDC Authentication.
# This file installs the extension; everything else -- discovery URL, client id,
# client secret, claim names, role mapping -- is a form in the web
# administrator, saved to the engine database with the secret encrypted.
#
# Install it by appending this to OIE_EXTENSION_URLS above, comma separated
# (the checksum is the release asset's, and it is enforced at boot):
#sha256:a18d208ca4e6ca0700790d971fde5b4212df70b3dc5b34ae3cc9032afdb3a5c2@https://github.com/gibson9583/oie-oidc-auth/releases/download/v1.0.1/oidcauth-1.0.1.zip
#
# Then sign in locally, fill the form in and tick Enabled. The login card grows
# a "Sign in with <provider>" button as soon as the engine reports the policy is
# configured; username-and-password sign-in never goes away.
#
# docs/sso.md has the Microsoft Entra ID recipe, including the app registration.

# Every setting also has an OIE_OIDC_* variable, and setting one here is a way
# to lose an afternoon: a variable PINS its field, which the console then shows
# read-only and will not save. They exist for deployments that configure
# machines rather than consoles -- if that is you, read the pinning note in
# docs/sso.md first. Otherwise leave them unset.

# The exception, because it is a refusal rather than a setting. It beats the
# stored policy, so it is how you turn SSO off when the provider is the thing
# that is broken -- and it needs the overlay, so that leaving it unset keeps the
# variable out of the container entirely:
#
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.sso-killswitch.yaml up -d
#
#OIE_OIDC_DISABLED=true

########################################################################
# Optional -- mTLS proxy overlay (compose.proxy.yaml)
########################################################################

#PROXY_API_PORT=443
#PROXY_HTTP_PORT=8444
#PROXY_MLLP_PORT=6662
#NGINX_IMAGE_TAG=1.29-alpine

########################################################################
# Optional -- values referenced by config/configuration-map.properties
########################################################################

#DOWNSTREAM_FHIR_URL=https://fhir.example.org
#DOWNSTREAM_FHIR_TOKEN=

########################################################################
# Optional -- running more than one engine (compose.cluster.yaml)
#
# Three engines against one database, behind one address: a deploy made on any
# of them is applied on all of them. Needs the Cluster extension installed
# (plugins/oie-cluster) and the shared keystore in place:
#
#   ./scripts/oie-cluster-keystore.sh
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.cluster.yaml up -d
#
# See docs/multi-pod.md. Nothing here has any effect on a single engine, and
# the extension changes nothing until OIE_CLUSTER_ENABLED is true.
########################################################################

#OIE_CLUSTER_NAME=local

# Per node, and set for you by compose.cluster.yaml / the Kubernetes manifests:
#
#   OIE_CLUSTER_ENABLED=true        apply cluster intent on this node
#   OIE_CLUSTER_ROLE=worker         or utility (the default); utility runs the
#                                   channels placed SINGLETON and the pruner
#   OIE_CLUSTER_NODE_NAME=...       what the console calls this node
#   OIE_CLUSTER_ADDRESS=https://... a per-node link for the console, if you
#                                   publish one
#   KEYSTORE_SOURCE=/run/secrets/keystore.jks
#   SESSION_STORE=true              sessions in PostgreSQL, so a round-robin
#   _MP_SERVER_API_SESSIONCACHE=none   front door does not need affinity
#   _MP_CONFIGURATIONMAP_LOCATION=database
#   SERVER_STARTUP_DEPLOY=false     the cluster decides what is deployed

# Which extensions the worker nodes do NOT get. Anything that does scheduled
# work from settings in the shared configuration table would otherwise do it
# once per pod: the engine's own data pruner, git sync, the volume monitor,
# Sentinel. The utility node keeps them.
#OIE_WORKER_DISABLE_EXTENSIONS=datapruner,gitsync,volumemonitor

# How long `oie-config-push.sh --wait-cluster` waits for every node to deploy
# what it just pushed, when no number is given.
#OIE_CLUSTER_WAIT=180

# server.id style for nodes that have not started yet. `name` makes the id the
# node's own name, which is what the message browser's "Server Id" column shows
# -- so every message, and every destination, says which node handled it without
# anyone having to translate a UUID. `uuid` (the default) keeps the traditional
# shape and folds OIE_CLUSTER_NAME in, which is what you want if two clusters
# ever share one database.
#OIE_SERVER_ID_STYLE=name
